<!-- Chapter 3 answers.

     Structure: an <exercises> division, so every level is a dropdown.
       exercise  -> dropdown (publication knowl/@exercise-divisional="yes")
       task      -> dropdown (publication knowl/@task="yes")
       solution  -> dropdown (always born hidden in PreTeXt)

     Exercise numbering restarts inside each <exercises> division, so the
     numbers here line up with ch3-exercises.ptx automatically. Keep the
     order of <exercise> elements identical to that file.
  -->

<exercises xml:id="ch3-answers" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Answers to Ch. 3 Exercises</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Answers to the exercises of this chapter. Click an
      exercise to open it, click a part to open that part, then click
      <term>Solution</term> to reveal the answer.
    </p>
    <p>
      These are the answers to the questions, not fully worked solutions. Enough is shown that you can
      tell whether your own work got to the correct conclusion.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="ans-hw-nash-definition">
    <title>The Nash Equilibrium Definition</title>

    <task xml:id="ans-nash-definition-math">
      <title>Nash equilibrium, in notation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          State mathematically what it means for <m>s^*</m> to be a Nash
          equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          For each <m>i = 1, \ldots, N</m> and every <m>s_i \in S_i</m>,
          <me>\pi_i(s^*) \geq \pi_i(s_i, s^*_{-i}).</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ans-nash-definition-not">
      <title>Not a Nash equilibrium</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          State mathematically what it means for <m>s^*</m> to not be a
          Nash equilibrium, and explain it in words.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          There is some player <m>i</m> and some <m>s_i \in S_i</m> with
          <me>\pi_i(s_i, s^*_{-i}) \gt \pi_i(s^*).</me>
          In words: at least one player can strictly improve by changing
          strategy alone. One such player and one such strategy are enough.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ans-nash-definition-matrix">
      <title>Three profiles in a <m>3 \times 3</m> game</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine whether <m>(a, x)</m>, <m>(b, y)</m>, and
          <m>(c, z)</m> are Nash equilibria, and whether each equilibrium
          is strict.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>(a, x)</m> is a strict Nash equilibrium. If player 1 switches, their payoff drops from 4 to 2 or 1. If player 2 switches, their payoff drops from 3 to 1 or 2.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>(b, y)</m> is not a Nash equilibrium. Player 1 can switch to <m>c</m> and get 6 instead of 5. (Player 2 can also improve, by switching to <m>z</m> for 6, but one player is enough.)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>(c, z)</m> is a Nash equilibrium, but not strict. Neither player can do better by switching, but player 2 gets 3 at either <m>x</m> or <m>z</m>, so switching to <m>x</m> does not make them strictly worse off.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="ans-hw-water-pollution-five">
    <title>Water Pollution with Five Firms</title>

    <task xml:id="ans-water-pollution-five-k">
      <title>Each number of polluters</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each <m>k = 0, \ldots, 5</m>, determine whether profiles with
          exactly <m>k</m> polluters are Nash equilibria.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>k = 0</m> or <m>1</m>: Not a Nash equilibrium. A purifier can switch to polluting, and the lake stays clean, so their payoff rises from <m>-1</m> to <m>0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>k = 2</m>: A Nash equilibrium. A polluter who switches to purifying drops from <m>0</m> to <m>-1</m>. A purifier who switches to polluting fouls the lake and drops from <m>-1</m> to <m>-3</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>k = 3</m>: Not a Nash equilibrium. A polluter can switch to purifying, which cleans the lake, so their payoff rises from <m>-3</m> to <m>-1</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>k = 4</m>: Not a Nash equilibrium. The purifier can switch to polluting. The lake stays fouled either way, so their payoff rises from <m>-4</m> to <m>-3</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>k = 5</m>: A Nash equilibrium. A firm that switches to purifying leaves 4 polluters, so the lake stays fouled and its payoff drops from <m>-3</m> to <m>-4</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ans-water-pollution-five-all">
      <title>All Nash equilibria</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe all Nash equilibria and count them.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Every profile in which exactly 2 firms pollute, plus the profile
          in which all 5 pollute. That is
          <m>\binom{5}{2} + 1 = 11</m> Nash equilibria.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>


  ==============================================================
  <exercise xml:id="ans-hw-bank-location-nash">
    <title>Locating Two Banks, Revisited</title>

    <task xml:id="ans-bank-location-nash-center">
      <title><m>(50, 50)</m> is a Nash equilibrium</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show directly that <m>(50, 50)</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          At <m>(50, 50)</m> each bank has <m>4950</m>. If <m>A</m> moves
          to any <m>p \neq 50</m>, <m>A</m> gets at most <m>4900</m>
          (achieved at <m>p = 49</m> or <m>p = 51</m>). So
          <m>\pi_A(p, 50) \leq 4900 \lt 4950</m>, and by symmetry the same
          holds for <m>B</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ans-bank-location-nash-apart">
      <title>Profiles with <m>p \lt q</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that no profile <m>(p, q)</m> with <m>p \lt q</m> is a Nash
          equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>q \geq p + 2</m>: Bank <m>A</m> can move to <m>p + 1</m>.
              It is still to the left of bank <m>B</m>, and it gains 50
              customers.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>q = p + 1</m>: Bank <m>A</m> has <m>100p</m> customers and
              bank <m>B</m> has <m>100(99 - p)</m>.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  If <m>p \leq 49</m>, bank <m>A</m> has at most
                  <m>4900</m>. It can move onto block <m>q</m> and get
                  <m>4950</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  If <m>p \geq 50</m>, bank <m>B</m> has at most
                  <m>4900</m>. It can move onto block <m>p</m> and get
                  <m>4950</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ans-bank-location-nash-same">
      <title>Profiles with <m>p = q \neq 50</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that no profile <m>(p, p)</m> with <m>p \neq 50</m> is a
          Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Each bank has <m>4950</m>. If <m>p \lt 50</m>, <m>A</m> moves to
          <m>p + 1</m> and gets blocks <m>p+1</m> through <m>99</m>, for
          <m>100(99 - p) \geq 5000</m>. If <m>p \gt 50</m>, <m>A</m> moves
          to <m>p - 1</m> and gets <m>100(p - 1) \geq 5000</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  ==============================================================
  <exercise xml:id="ans-hw-resource-game">
    <title>Bidding the Resource</title>

    <task xml:id="ans-resource-game-one-one">
      <title><m>(1, 1)</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>(1, 1)</m> is a Nash equilibrium, and decide whether
          it is strict.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\pi_1(1, 1) = 0</m>. Any <m>s_1 \lt 1</m> gives
          <m>s_1 \lt s_2</m>, so <m>\pi_1(s_1, 1) = 0 \leq 0</m>. The same
          holds for player 2 by symmetry, so <m>(1, 1)</m> is a Nash
          equilibrium.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is <em>not</em> strict: deviations give exactly <m>0</m>, not
          strictly less.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ans-resource-game-below-one">
      <title><m>s_2 \lt s_1 \lt 1</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that player 2 can improve.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Player 2 currently gets <m>0</m>. Switching to any <m>s_2'</m>
          with <m>s_1 \lt s_2' \leq 1</m> makes player 2 the winner, with
          payoff <m>(1 - s_1) + (1 - s_2') \geq 1 - s_1 \gt 0</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ans-resource-game-at-one">
      <title><m>s_2 \lt s_1 = 1</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain why player 2's deviation no longer works, and show the
          profile is still not a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Player 2 cannot bid more than 1, and tying at <m>1</m> gives
          player 2 a payoff of <m>1 - 1 = 0</m>, which is no improvement.
        </p>
        <p>
          Player 1 improves instead: currently <m>\pi_1 = 1 - s_2</m>.
          Switching to any <m>s_1'</m> with <m>s_2 \lt s_1' \lt 1</m> keeps
          player 1 the winner, with payoff
          <m>(1 - s_1') + (1 - s_2) \gt 1 - s_2</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ans-resource-game-tie">
      <title><m>s_1 = s_2 \lt 1</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show there are no Nash equilibria with <m>s_1 = s_2 \lt 1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Write <m>s_1 = s_2 = s</m>, so player 1 gets <m>1 - s</m>.
          Switching to any <m>s_1'</m> with <m>s \lt s_1' \lt 1</m> gives
          player 1 <m>(1 - s_1') + (1 - s) \gt 1 - s</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
<!--
  ==============================================================
  <exercise xml:id="ans-hw-hawk-dove-nash">
    <title>Hawk and Dove, Revisited</title>

    <task xml:id="ans-hawk-dove-nash-dominated">
      <title>Dominated strategies</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Identify any dominated strategies when <m>0 \lt v \lt w</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Neither strategy is dominated. If the other player plays
          <m>h</m>, then <m>d</m> is better, since
          <m>0 \gt \frac{v-w}{2}</m>. If the other player plays <m>d</m>,
          then <m>h</m> is better, since <m>v \gt \frac{v}{2}</m>. Each
          strategy does better against something, so neither one dominates
          the other, weakly or strictly.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ans-hawk-dove-nash-find">
      <title>All Nash equilibria</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all Nash equilibria when <m>0 \lt v \lt w</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The only Nash equilibria are <m>(h, d)</m> and <m>(d, h)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ans-hawk-dove-nash-words">
      <title>Explaining in words</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain informally why neither animal wants to switch.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          At <m>(h, d)</m>:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The hawk gets the whole territory without a fight. Switching
              to dove would mean only getting to share it.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The dove retreats and avoids injury. Switching to hawk would
              start a fight, and the risk of injury costs more than the
              territory is worth.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <m>(d, h)</m> is the same argument with the players swapped.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  ==============================================================
  <exercise xml:id="ans-hw-three-player-best-response">
    <title>A Three Player Game, Using Best Response</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use best responses to find all Nash equilibria.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        There are three Nash equilibria, listed as
        (player 1, player 2, player 3):
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><m>(A, A, B)</m> with payoffs <m>(20, 20, 20)</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>(B, A, A)</m> with payoffs <m>(20, 15, 10)</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>(B, B, B)</m> with payoffs <m>(30, 25, 30)</m></p></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These are the only cells with all three payoffs boxed.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  ==============================================================
  <exercise xml:id="ans-hw-pick-a-number">
    <title>Pick a Number</title>

    <task xml:id="ans-pick-a-number-nash">
      <title>Everyone choosing <m>k</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that every player choosing <m>k</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The average is <m>k</m>, so the target is <m>\frac{3k}{4}</m>.
          Every player is the same distance from it, so all tie and
          <m>\pi_i(k, \bar{k}) = 4</m>. Since 4 is the largest possible
          payoff, <m>\pi_i(s_i, \bar{k}) \leq 4 = \pi_i(k, \bar{k})</m>
          for every <m>s_i</m>, so this is a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ans-pick-a-number-strict">
      <title>When it is strict</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For which <m>k</m> is the equilibrium strict?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The equilibrium is strict only when <m>k = 1</m>.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>k = 1</m>: The target is <m>\frac{3}{4}</m>. Every other
              choice is larger, so it is farther from the target, and the
              player's payoff drops from 4 to 0. So the equilibrium is
              strict.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>k = 2</m>: The target is <m>\frac{3}{2}</m>. Choosing 1 is
              just as close to the target as 2, so the player still ties and
              gets 4. So the equilibrium is not strict.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>k \geq 3</m>: The integer closest to the target
              <m>\frac{3k}{4}</m> is not <m>k</m>. A player who switches to
              it wins outright and still gets 4. So the equilibrium is not
              strict.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  ==============================================================
  <exercise xml:id="ans-hw-first-price-auction">
    <title>A First-Price Auction</title>

    <task xml:id="ans-first-price-auction-nash">
      <title>The Nash equilibrium</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>b_1 = v_2</m> and <m>b_i = v_i</m> for
          <m>i \geq 2</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We need to check that no player can improve their payoff by
          switching their bid on their own.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Player 1: Since <m>h_1 = v_2</m>, player 1 ties player 2's bid
              and wins because of the lower index. Player 1's payoff is
              <m>v_1 - v_2 \geq 0</m>.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Bidding higher: Player 1 still wins, but pays more, so the
                  payoff goes down.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Bidding lower: Player 1 loses the auction, and the payoff
                  becomes <m>0</m>, which is no better than
                  <m>v_1 - v_2</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Player <m>i \geq 2</m>: Player <m>i</m> loses the auction, so
              their payoff is <m>0</m>.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Bidding lower: Player <m>i</m> still loses, and the payoff
                  stays <m>0</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Bidding higher: Player 1 bids <m>v_2</m> and wins ties, so
                  to win, player <m>i</m> must bid <m>b_i \gt v_2</m>. Then
                  the payoff is <m>v_i - b_i \lt v_i - v_2 \leq 0</m>, which
                  is worse than <m>0</m>. A higher bid that does not win
                  still pays <m>0</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          No player can improve, so this is a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ans-first-price-auction-above">
      <title>Bidding above your value</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show <m>b_i \gt v_i</m> is weakly dominated by
          <m>b_i' = v_i</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We need to check both conditions in the definition of a weakly
          dominated strategy.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Never worse: If player <m>i</m> bids <m>v_i</m>, the payoff is
              always <m>0</m>, because winning pays <m>v_i - v_i = 0</m> and
              losing pays <m>0</m>. If player <m>i</m> bids
              <m>b_i \gt v_i</m>, the payoff is either <m>v_i - b_i \lt 0</m>
              (a win) or <m>0</m> (a loss). So
              <m>\pi_i(v_i, b_{-i}) \geq \pi_i(b_i, b_{-i})</m> for every
              <m>b_{-i}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Sometimes strictly better: Suppose every other player bids
              <m>0</m>. Then the bid <m>b_i</m> wins, and player <m>i</m>
              gets <m>v_i - b_i \lt 0</m>, while bidding <m>v_i</m> gives
              <m>0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="ans-first-price-auction-at">
      <title>Bidding exactly your value</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show <m>b_i = v_i</m> is weakly dominated by any
          <m>0 \lt b_i' \lt v_i</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We need to check both conditions in the definition of a weakly
          dominated strategy.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Never worse: If player <m>i</m> bids <m>v_i</m>, the payoff is
              always <m>0</m>. If player <m>i</m> bids <m>b_i'</m> with
              <m>0 \lt b_i' \lt v_i</m>, the payoff is either
              <m>v_i - b_i' \gt 0</m> (a win) or <m>0</m> (a loss). So
              <m>\pi_i(b_i', b_{-i}) \geq \pi_i(v_i, b_{-i})</m> for every
              <m>b_{-i}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Sometimes strictly better: Suppose every other player bids
              <m>0</m>. Then the bid <m>b_i'</m> wins, and player <m>i</m>
              gets <m>v_i - b_i' \gt 0</m>, while bidding <m>v_i</m> gives
              <m>0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

-->

</exercises>
